/* Copyright 2026 Rhett Creighton - Apache License 2.0
 *
 * ZCL_CORE_SEAL_TREE + ZCL_CORE_SEAL_SECTION_ROWS — the sealed consensus core's
 * per-SECTION identity, as compile-time strings. AUTO-GENERATED by
 * `make core-seal-sections` (and by `make core-seal`) from the SECTION/TREE
 * lines of core/MANIFEST.sha3. Do not edit by hand.
 *
 * WHY IT IS NOT UNDER core/. Same reason as hotswap/core_seal_root.h: a mirror
 * living inside the sealed set would be part of its own input. It lives beside
 * the consumer, and test_hotswap_module's
 * "compiled section table matches core/MANIFEST.sha3" case proves the two
 * agree — a forgotten regeneration fails the hotswap_module test group.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS FOR. hotswap/core_seal_root.h carries ONE value: the flat ROOT
 * fold over every sealed file. It is a whole-tree pin — every module is
 * invalidated by a change to any of the 70 sealed files. These rows carry the
 * SAME sealed set at directory granularity, in cognition/modules/codeindex's source Merkle
 * dialect (a structurally different fold over the same bytes), so a module can
 * RECORD which sections it actually compiled against and the resident can
 * verify each of them IN ADDITION to ROOT.
 *
 * THIS FILE RELAXES NOTHING. The ROOT equality check is unchanged and still
 * runs first. A module that declares sections must ALSO match every one of
 * them and the TREE; a module that declares a section this resident does not
 * have is refused. The section rows are strictly additional evidence.
 *
 * The N-ary node digest for a directory covers that directory RECURSIVELY, so
 * an ancestor row ("core") subsumes its descendants. A module declares the
 * DEEPEST section containing each sealed file in its compile closure and never
 * its ancestors — an ancestor digest would pin sibling directories the module
 * never saw, which is the opposite of what section granularity is for.
 */

